-- ORIGIN AGREEMENT probe (#1110) — do the compiler's THREE elaboration drivers
-- agree about which module declared each type-constructor head?
--
--   medaka build --allow-internal compiler/entries/origin_agreement_main.mdk -o <bin>
--   <bin> <runtime.mdk> <core.mdk> <entry.mdk> [root ...]
--
-- ── why this exists ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `Ty.TyCon` carries a `TyConOrigin` stamped by resolve on both the check path and
-- the emit path, and so do the four TYPE-DECLARATION nodes (`DData.dataOrigin`,
-- `DNewtype.newtypeOrigin`, `DTypeAlias.tyAliasOrigin`, `DInterface.ifaceOrigin`).
-- Nothing in the compiler reads any of them, which is deliberate — but it
-- also means every identity fact the compiler mints is UNOBSERVABLE, and two
-- S0-class defects shipped through 12/12 green CI on exactly that:
--
--   F1  the flat driver stamped `mod:__user__` for a file's own declarations while
--       the emitter's graph path stamped the real loader id — INSIDE ONE PROCESS.
--   F2  on prelude-flattened paths the PRELUDE's own types (`Option`, `Result`,
--       `Ordering`) were attributed to the USER's module.
--
-- ⚠️ NEITHER IS CATCHABLE BY A PROBE THAT CALLS THE STAMPERS DIRECTLY.
-- `stampFlatTyOrigins` was correct for its arguments in both cases; the bug was a
-- CALLER — a hardcoded literal at one of three call sites (F1), and an empty prelude
-- list (F2).  A probe that hand-picks its arguments re-encodes the assumption it
-- exists to test and reports green.  So this drives the REAL entry points, the way
-- the real drivers drive them, and reads back what THEY produced:
--
--   flat    checkProgramSchemesWithRuntime   `medaka check` on a no-import file, the
--                                            LSP, `doc`, `repl`, the playground —
--                                            one buffer at a time, prelude separate.
--                                            Its result is SCHEMES, i.e. `Mono`,
--                                            not the stamped decl trees — so it is
--                                            read through resolve.mdk's agreement
--                                            tap.  (`Mono.TCon` gained an origin
--                                            field in #1110's unit D; this probe
--                                            still reads the DECL trees, because
--                                            the schemes only cover heads that
--                                            reach a top-level binding's type.)
--   single  elaborateModules … [(id, …)]      `medaka test <file>`
--                                            (compiler/tools/test_cmd.mdk's
--                                            `runSingle`/`runPropsSingle`/
--                                            `runTestDeclsSingle`/
--                                            `propsReportSingle` — NOT the
--                                            snapshot driver, `tools/snapshot.mdk`,
--                                            which has its own SEPARATE
--                                            `"__user__"` call site, untouched by
--                                            ARCH E-5 and unmodelled by this arm).
--                                            `id` is a DIRECT CALL to
--                                            `test_cmd.mdk`'s own EXPORTED
--                                            `singleRootId`, over roots this arm
--                                            still independently derives from
--                                            THIS module's own file path (see
--                                            `singleArm`'s comment for what that
--                                            residual review-dependence covers)
--                                            — since ARCH E-5 (#1521) retired the
--                                            synthetic `"__user__"` literal
--                                            (#1223).
--   graph   elaborateModules … <loader graph>
--                                            `medaka run` / `medaka build` /
--                                            `medaka test <dir>`.  Reads the trees
--                                            elaborateModules RETURNS — what the
--                                            emitter actually consumes — so a later
--                                            pass that DESTROYS an acquired origin
--                                            shows up as a claim that vanished.
--
-- ── why the OUTPUT is an agreement table and not the origins ──────────────────
-- A golden of the origins themselves would (a) churn on every module added and
-- (b) NOT have caught F1: each arm's claim was individually plausible, and only the
-- DISAGREEMENT was wrong.  An agreement table detects a disagreement without anyone
-- having to know the right answer — the only property that survives an arc whose
-- whole point is that the right answer is not decided yet.
--
-- ⚠️ The current asymmetry is DELIBERATE and must NOT read as a failure: the flat
-- path is SILENT (`OriginUnresolved`) where it has no loader-derived module id,
-- rather than inventing one.  That silence IS F1's fix — see stampFlatTyOrigins'
-- own comment.  So "one arm silent, the other claims" is `L-ONLY`/`R-ONLY` and is
-- fine; "both claim, differently" is `CONFLICT` and is the defect class.
--
-- 🚨 AND HERE IS THE HONEST BOUND ON ALL OF THAT — the flip side of the property
-- that makes the design good.  PAIRWISE AGREEMENT RESTS ON AT LEAST ONE ARM BEING
-- RIGHT.  Where the three arms share an input, that premise fails and a UNIFORM
-- wrong answer has no disagreement left to expose: relabel the prelude at BOTH of
-- resolve.mdk's `"core"` sites, or point every builtin at a module that does not
-- exist, and all three arms tell the same wrong story — every verdict stays AGREE
-- and the table is byte-identical.  Review found exactly that, and it is F2's
-- literal symptom (the prelude's own types attributed elsewhere) getting through.
--
-- `HEAD SPREAD` is what closes it, and ONLY because it prints the claim KEYS rather
-- than counting them.  A uniform wrong story is still ONE story, so no count moves;
-- the origin itself has to be on the page.  It is the single place in this output
-- where a concrete origin is asserted for every head rather than only for the ones
-- already known to disagree, and it is a per-NAME assertion (~17 lines) rather than
-- a per-row one, so it pins the answer without the churn a full origins golden has.
-- It also upgrades the re-export check from "one story" to "ALPHA's story", which is
-- what that section was always trying to say.
--
-- Slots are the probe's OWN bookkeeping — the loader path it read each decl list
-- from — never an id the compiler minted.  That is what lets a driver that invents
-- an id show up as a disagreeing CLAIM rather than as a quietly relabelled row.
--
-- ── the RESIDUAL section: what agreement structurally cannot say ─────────────
-- Everything above compares arms to each other, so it is blind in one direction by
-- construction: a head NO arm attributed has nothing to disagree about, and reads
-- as a placid `NEITHER`.  `OriginUnresolved` surviving the stamping pass is exactly
-- that shape, and `stampTyHead`'s immunity rule (fill in only a still-unresolved
-- head) is what makes it matter — the first stamp is the ONLY stamp, so a head that
-- reaches a consumer unstamped stays unstamped for the rest of the pipeline.
--
-- `=== RESIDUAL ===` prints that surviving set per arm, by name.  It is the runtime
-- half of #1110's drain: the producer ratchet in `test/typecheck_compiler_source.sh`
-- pins who may MINT the sentinel (a fact about the source) and says in its own
-- comment that it "cannot see whether a driver forgot to stamp, nor whether one
-- stamped the WRONG id".  CONFLICT answers the second; this answers the first.
--
-- 🚨 IT IS NOT ASSERTED EMPTY, AND MUST NOT BE.  The flat arm is silent about a
-- large, DELIBERATE, documented set (subset-and-agreement — see the section's own
-- comment further down).  Only the pinning is the assertion.

import frontend.ast.{Decl(..), Ty(..), TyConOrigin(..)}
import frontend.parser.{parse}
import frontend.desugar.{desugar}
import frontend.resolve.{
  setOriginTrace,
  takeOriginTrace,
  mapOriginsInDecl,
  ifaceKey,
}
import types.typecheck.{elaborateModules, checkProgramSchemesWithRuntime}
import entries.entry_support.{failWith, usageModules}
import driver.loader.{loadProgramFilesE, loadErrorMessage, entrySearchRoots}
import tools.test_cmd.{singleRootId}
import support.util.{listLen}
import support.path.{dirOf}

-- ── claims ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- (arm, slot, head name, origin key).  Positional on purpose: record field names
-- are a program-global namespace in this language (#1216) and this probe needs none.
data Claim = Claim String String String String

claimArm : Claim -> String
claimArm (Claim a _ _ _) = a

claimSlot : Claim -> String
claimSlot (Claim _ s _ _) = s

claimName : Claim -> String
claimName (Claim _ _ n _) = n

claimKey : Claim -> String
claimKey (Claim _ _ _ k) = k

-- `-` is ABSENCE OF A CLAIM, not a claim of "nothing".  Per resolve.mdk: no
-- predicate may treat two `OriginUnresolved` heads as matching each other.
originKey : TyConOrigin -> String
originKey OriginUnresolved = "-"
originKey OriginBuiltin = "builtin"
originKey (OriginModule m) = "mod:\{m}"

-- ── driver ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- ⚠️ NOT `entries.entry_support.driveModules` — deliberately, since ARCH E-5
-- (#1521/#1223 blocker 2 review).  `driveModules` calls `loadProgram`, which
-- drops each module's FILE PATH, keeping only (mid, decls).  The `single` arm
-- below needs the real path to compute its id the way `test_cmd.mdk`'s fixed
-- `singleRootId` does (`entrySearchRoots (dirOf path)` — recomputed PER MODULE,
-- mirroring `medaka_cli.mdk`'s `testFilesGo`, which derives each test target's
-- roots from THAT target's own directory, not the probe's outer CLI roots) — so
-- this driver calls `loadProgramFilesE` directly, the path-preserving loader
-- entry point `loadProgram` itself wraps, and threads the (mid, path, decls)
-- triples through to `singleArm` instead of discarding the path.
main : <IO> Unit
main = match args ()
  runtimeP::coreP::entry::roots0 =>
    driveGo runtimeP coreP entry (rootsOrDefaultHere entry roots0)
  _ => failWith usageModules

-- Local copy of entry_support's private `rootsOrDefault` (not exported there).
rootsOrDefaultHere : String -> List String -> List String
rootsOrDefaultHere entry [] = [dirOf entry]
rootsOrDefaultHere _ roots = roots

driveGo : String -> String -> String -> List String -> <IO> Unit
driveGo runtimeP coreP entry roots = match readFile runtimeP
  Err e => failWith e
  Ok rsrc => match readFile coreP
    Err e => failWith e
    Ok csrc => match loadProgramFilesE (_ => None) entry roots
      Err e => failWith (loadErrorMessage e)
      Ok triples => report (dirOf runtimeP) (desugar (parse rsrc)) (desugar (parse csrc)) (map desugarTriple triples)

desugarTriple : (String, String, List Decl) -> (String, String, List Decl)
desugarTriple (mid, path, p) = (mid, path, desugar p)

dropPath : (String, String, List Decl) -> (String, List Decl)
dropPath (mid, _, decls) = (mid, decls)

report : String -> List Decl -> List Decl -> List (String, String, List Decl) -> <IO> Unit
report stdlibDir runtimeDecls coreDecls triples =
  let modules = map dropPath triples
  let (segments, flatClaims) = flatArm runtimeDecls coreDecls modules
  let singleClaims = singleArm runtimeDecls coreDecls stdlibDir triples
  let (graphMods, graphDecls, graphIfaces, graphClaims) = graphArm runtimeDecls coreDecls modules
  let allClaims = flatClaims ++ singleClaims ++ graphClaims
  let uniq = dedupAdj sameClaim (sortByCmp cmpClaim allClaims)
  let (table, conflicts, nrows, nconf) = groupRows uniq
  let (residual, nresid) = residualSection uniq
  let _ = putStr "=== ORIGIN AGREEMENT (#1110) ===\n"
  let _ = putStr "# <slot> <head> <flat~graph> <single~graph> <flat~single>\n"
  let _ = putStr table
  let _ = putStr "=== CONFLICTS ===\n"
  let _ = putStr "# ARCH E-5 (#1521) PARTIALLY fixed #1223 — the NO-IMPORT case only.\n"
  let _ = putStr "# compiler/tools/test_cmd.mdk's single-file drivers now compute\n"
  let _ = putStr "# `canonicalPathId` (last-containing-root, round-trip-guarded) instead of a\n"
  let _ = putStr "# hardcoded \"__user__\" literal, via the EXPORTED `singleRootId`. This probe's\n"
  let _ = putStr "# `single` arm CALLS that same exported function directly (not by reusing the\n"
  let _ = putStr "# graph arm's `mid` as its elaboration id, which would manufacture AGREE and\n"
  let _ = putStr "# prove nothing — see singleArm's own comment) — so a genuine single~graph\n"
  let _ = putStr "# CONFLICT is still REACHABLE here, and the DRIVER regressing back to plain\n"
  let _ = putStr "# `moduleIdOfPath` inside `singleRootId` would flip this gate red.\n"
  let _ = putStr "# ⚠️ THE ROOTS LIST FED TO `singleRootId` IS STILL THIS ARM'S OWN MODEL of\n"
  let _ = putStr "# `testFilesGo` (`entrySearchRoots (dirOf path) ++ [stdlibDir]`), not a call\n"
  let _ = putStr "# to it — see `singleArm`'s comment for what that residual gap covers.\n"
  let _ = putStr "# The rows #1223 pinned (single-root corpus) read AGREE, and a nested-project\n"
  let _ = putStr "# row (test/origin_fixtures/nested) requires the last-root convention to\n"
  let _ = putStr "# agree, REGARDLESS of directory nesting (that fixture is cross-directory).\n"
  let _ = putStr "# #1223 stays OPEN: an IMPORT-BEARING file's ENTRY-vs-DEPENDENCY id asymmetry\n"
  let _ = putStr "# (loader.mdk:662-669, never touched by this fix) still reproduces — pinned in\n"
  let _ = putStr "# this gate's dedicated entry_residual section, not this table.\n"
  let _ = putStr conflicts
  let _ = putStr "=== RESIDUAL ===\n"
  let _ = putStr "# <arm> <slot> <head> — an `OriginUnresolved` head that SURVIVED stamping.\n"
  let _ = putStr "# NOT necessarily a defect; NOT necessarily empty. Read the section comment.\n"
  let _ = putStr residual
  let _ = putStr "=== HEAD SPREAD ===\n"
  let _ = putStr "# <head> <distinct graph-arm origins across ALL slots> <the origins themselves>\n"
  let _ = putStr (spreadSection uniq)
  let _ = putStr "=== SUMMARY ===\n"
  -- What each number counts, so a reviewer checking the arithmetic on a golden diff
  -- does not have to reconstruct the claim model from this file:
  --   heads       TABLE ROWS — distinct (slot, head) pairs, all arms merged.
  --   claims      distinct (arm, slot, head, originKey) tuples AFTER dedup, so ONE
  --               head claimed identically by all three arms contributes 3, and a
  --               head an arm never saw contributes 0 for that arm.
  --   residual    distinct (arm, slot, head) with NO origin — a subset of `claims`.
  --   conflicts   TABLE ROWS carrying a CONFLICT in any of the three arm pairs.
  --   decl-heads / iface-occs-*   raw carrier SITES the graph arm visited (not
  --               deduped, not row counts) — the anti-vacuity signals.
  let _ = putStr "modules \{listLen modules}\n"
  let _ = putStr "graph-modules \{graphMods}\n"
  let _ = putStr "flat-segments \{segments}\n"
  let _ = putStr "decl-heads \{graphDecls}\n"
  let _ = putStr (carrierLines graphIfaces)
  let _ = putStr "heads \{nrows}\n"
  let _ = putStr "claims \{listLen uniq}\n"
  let _ = putStr "residual \{nresid}\n"
  putStr "conflicts \{nconf}\n"

-- ── the three arms ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- FLAT: one buffer at a time, exactly as the LSP / `doc` / `check` drivers call it.
-- Returns (how many times the tap fired, claims).  The segment count is the
-- anti-vacuity signal for the TAP ITSELF: if a future refactor stops routing the
-- flat drivers through `checkProgramSeededSplit`, this goes to 0 and the gate reds —
-- rather than the table quietly losing its `flat` column while still reporting
-- "0 conflicts".
flatArm : List Decl -> List Decl -> List (String, List Decl) -> (Int, List Claim)
flatArm _ _ [] = (0, [])
flatArm rt core ((mid, decls)::rest) =
  let _ = setOriginTrace True
  let _ = checkProgramSchemesWithRuntime rt core decls
  let trace = takeOriginTrace ()
  let _ = setOriginTrace False
  let (n, cs) = flatArm rt core rest
  (listLen trace + n, flatClaimsOf mid trace ++ cs)

-- The flat driver labels its two halves "core" and "user".  The user half is the
-- buffer THIS probe handed it, so its slot is the loader path the probe read —
-- never a name the compiler chose.
flatClaimsOf : String -> List (String, List Decl) -> List Claim
flatClaimsOf _ [] = []
flatClaimsOf mid ((label, decls)::rest) = claimsOf "flat" (flatSlot mid label) decls
  ++ flatClaimsOf mid rest

flatSlot : String -> String -> String
flatSlot _ "core" = "core"
flatSlot mid _ = mid

-- SINGLE: `medaka test <file>` elaborates ONE file as a 1-module graph.  Driven
-- here over the SAME decls the graph arm gets, which is precisely the
-- `medaka test <dir>` situation: one declaration, and — before ARCH E-5 (#1521)
-- fixed it — two module ids in one process (#1223).
--
-- ⚠️ A probe that hand-picks its own argument here re-encodes the assumption it
-- exists to test and reports green — this is the same hazard the stampers'
-- header argues against, turned on one of this probe's own arms, and it is
-- exactly what the PREVIOUS version of this arm did: it reused `mid`, the
-- GRAPH arm's own bookkeeping id, as BOTH the slot AND the elaboration id, which
-- manufactures AGREE by construction (single's claim and graph's claim are then
-- both `mod:{mid}`, always, regardless of what `test_cmd.mdk` actually computes)
-- and can never produce a CONFLICT — caught in review (#1526 blocker 2).
--
-- Fixed (#1526 blocker 2, PREFERRED remedy — call the driver, don't model it):
-- this arm now calls `tools.test_cmd.singleRootId` DIRECTLY — the SAME exported
-- function `runSingle`/`runPropsSingle`/`runTestDeclsSingle`/`propsReportSingle`
-- call — over roots freshly derived from the module's OWN file path
-- (`entrySearchRoots (dirOf path) ++ [stdlibDir]`, mirroring `medaka_cli.mdk`'s
-- `testFilesGo`, which computes each test target's roots from THAT target's own
-- directory) — using `mid` ONLY as the table SLOT (the probe's bookkeeping key,
-- shared with flat/graph so their claims about the same structural module land
-- in one row), never as the computed id itself.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS ARM STILL DOES NOT CALL `test_cmd.mdk` END TO END, AND THAT RESIDUE IS
-- NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. It calls the driver's OWN id-derivation function, so
-- a future change to HOW THE ID IS COMPUTED is caught automatically (mechanical,
-- not review-dependent, for that half). It still INDEPENDENTLY RECOMPUTES THE
-- ROOTS LIST above — mirroring `testFilesGo` rather than calling it — so a
-- future change to HOW ROOTS ARE DERIVED for a test target (e.g. a third search
-- path added to `entrySearchRoots` or `testFilesGo` itself) would NOT be caught
-- here automatically; that slice still drains by review, not mechanically.
--
-- This arm can genuinely disagree with the graph arm, and does whenever a module
-- sits below its own `medaka.toml`, regardless of directory nesting
-- (`test/origin_fixtures/nested`, added alongside the underlying fix because the
-- pre-existing corpus — all single-root — could not exercise the divergence
-- `canonicalPathId` exists to close; see that fixture's own comment).
-- `stdlibDir` is `dirOf runtimeP`, this probe's own first CLI arg
-- (`stdlib/runtime.mdk` in every real invocation, so its `dirOf` is exactly the
-- same `stdlibDir` the real CLI computes independently).
singleArm : List Decl -> List Decl -> String -> List (String, String, List Decl) -> <IO> List Claim
singleArm _ _ _ [] = []
singleArm rt core stdlibDir ((mid, path, decls)::rest) =
  let sRoots = entrySearchRoots (dirOf path) ++ [stdlibDir]
  let rootId = singleRootId sRoots path
  let (coreD, mods) = elaborateModules rt core [(rootId, decls)]
  claimsOf "single" "core" coreD
    ++ claimsOf "single" mid (flatMap snd mods)
    ++ singleArm rt core stdlibDir rest

-- GRAPH: the loader's whole module graph, elaborated once — `run` / `build`.
-- Returns (module count, DECL-LAYER carriers seen, PER-CARRIER interface-occurrence
-- counts, claims).  The two middle values are the anti-vacuity signals for the two
-- layers no `Ty` walk can reach, the exact peers of `flat-segments` for the tap: if
-- a future refactor stops those carriers reaching the trees `elaborateModules`
-- returns, a count goes to 0 and the gate reds — rather than the table quietly
-- losing every `decl:` / `iface:` row while still reporting "0 conflicts".
graphArm : List Decl -> List Decl -> List (String, List Decl) -> (Int, Int, List (String, Int), List Claim)
graphArm rt core modules =
  let (coreD, mods) = elaborateModules rt core modules
  let cs = claimsOf "graph" "core" coreD ++ graphModClaims (map fst modules) mods
  (
    listLen mods,
    listLen (declHeads coreD) + declHeadsCount mods,
    carrierCounts coreD mods,
    cs,
  )

-- Slots come from the INPUT list, positionally.  The probe must not take the
-- driver's word for which module a returned decl list belongs to.
graphModClaims : List String -> List (String, List Decl) -> List Claim
graphModClaims [] _ = []
graphModClaims _ [] = []
graphModClaims (slot::slots) ((_, decls)::rest) = claimsOf "graph" slot decls
  ++ graphModClaims slots rest

claimsOf : String -> String -> List Decl -> List Claim
claimsOf arm slot decls = map (headClaim arm slot) (headsOf decls)

headClaim : String -> String -> (String, TyConOrigin) -> Claim
headClaim arm slot (n, o) = Claim arm slot n (originKey o)

-- ── the head collector ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- THREE LAYERS, and the distinction is the whole reason this probe was widened.
--
--   OCCURRENCE  every `TyCon` head written in a signature, stamped by resolve's
--               `stampTyOrigins`/`stampTyHead`.  Collected through
--               `mapOriginsInDecl` — the SAME total traversal the stamper uses
--               (`stampDeclTyOrigins`).  Sharing the traversal is the point: this
--               probe can never observe a SMALLER set of positions than the
--               stamper wrote, and a new Ty position added to the AST reaches
--               both at once.
--   DECLARATION `DData.dataOrigin` / `DNewtype.newtypeOrigin` /
--               `DTypeAlias.tyAliasOrigin` / `DInterface.ifaceOrigin`, stamped by
--               resolve's `stampDeclOrigins`/`stampDeclOrigin`.
--   INTERFACE   `Constraint.constraintOrigin` (a `=>` predicate),
--   OCCURRENCE  `Super.superOrigin` (`interface Sub a requires Sup a`),
--               `Require.requireOrigin` (`impl I T requires Eq a`) and
--               `DImpl.implOrigin` (the interface an `impl` is an impl OF),
--               stamped by resolve's `fillIfaceOccOrigin` through
--               `mapOriginsInDecl`, and collected here through that SAME function,
--               which is why this probe can never observe a smaller set of
--               positions than the stamper wrote.
--
-- 🚨 THE DECL LAYER WAS INVISIBLE HERE UNTIL THIS CHANGE, AND THAT IS EXACTLY THE
-- CONDITION UNDER WHICH THE TWO S0s IN THIS FILE'S HEADER SHIPPED THROUGH GREEN CI.
-- `mapTyInDecl` rewrites `Ty` POSITIONS; a `…Origin` field is a field on the DECL
-- NODE, not a `Ty`, so no callback handed to it can ever see one.  The four
-- carriers landed populated and graded by nothing — a carrier no gate reads is the
-- precondition for every defect this gate exists to catch.  So the decl layer is
-- collected by its OWN walk, beside the occurrence one, and not by widening
-- `noteHead` (which is a `Ty -> (Ty, Bool)` callback and structurally cannot).
--
-- ⚠️ THE LAYERS ARE STAMPED BY DIFFERENT FUNCTIONS ON DIFFERENT PATHS, so they
-- must stay TELLABLE APART in the output or a decl-layer regression hides behind an
-- occurrence-layer row of the same name.  A decl carrier is reported under the name
-- `decl:<Name>`, an interface occurrence under `iface:<Name>`; a bare `<TypeName>`
-- row is always a type occurrence.  `:` cannot occur in a Medaka type or interface
-- name, so the three namespaces cannot collide.
--
-- ⚠️ The layers still disagree today, by design, and the golden shows it — but
-- narrower than it used to be (#1227, fixed): the FLAT driver's
-- `checkProgramSeededSplit` (`types/typecheck.mdk`) now calls `stampDeclOrigins
-- "core"` on its PRELUDE half, alongside `stampFlatTyOrigins`'s occurrence stamp,
-- so `flat core decl:*` rows AGREE with the graph arm.  It still never stamps the
-- USER half, or any flat driver with no separate prelude boundary
-- (`coreProg0 = []`, e.g. `checkProgramSeeded`) — those have no module id, so
-- their `decl:` rows stay silent, and that silence is the documented
-- `#1110 flat-identity` residual, not a defect.
-- 🚨 INTERFACE ROWS CARRY AN `iface:` PREFIX, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS
-- REQUIREMENT, NOT A LABELLING PREFERENCE.  Medaka keeps type names and interface
-- names in SEPARATE NAMESPACES — a file declaring both `data Foo = Foo` and
-- `interface Foo a where …` checks clean — so an interface `Foo` pushed into this
-- accumulator under the BARE name `Foo` would land on the same key as a type `Foo`
-- and be graded AGREE/CONFLICT against it.  Both sides are typed `TyConOrigin`, so
-- that comparison is type-correct and therefore invisible: the shared carrier type
-- is exactly what removes the compiler's ability to object.  In one arm and slot it
-- would read as `ArmMulti` — an arm contradicting itself — i.e. a CONFLICT
-- manufactured by the probe's own keying.
--
-- ⚠️ The DECL layer's `decl:` keys still merge the two namespaces (`decl:Foo` for a
-- `data Foo` and for an `interface Foo` alike), and that is left alone deliberately
-- rather than overlooked: every `decl:` row in one slot is stamped with THAT
-- module's id, so a type/interface pun inside one module yields two IDENTICAL
-- claims, dedups to one row, and cannot move a verdict.  The residue is that a
-- `HEAD SPREAD` line can merge a type and an interface of the same name declared in
-- different modules; that section already documents `> 1` as corpus-dependent and
-- pinned for review.  Splitting it would move existing golden rows for no verdict
-- change, so it is stated here instead.
headsOf : List Decl -> List (String, TyConOrigin)
headsOf decls =
  let acc = Ref []
  let _ = walkDecls acc decls
  declHeads decls ++ !acc

-- ONE walk, BOTH occurrence layers, through resolve's own `mapOriginsInDecl` — the
-- very function `stampDeclTyOrigins` drives.  Both recorders share one accumulator
-- because their keys are already disjoint: a type head goes in bare, an interface
-- occurrence goes in `iface:`-tagged.
walkDecls : Ref (List (String, TyConOrigin)) -> List Decl -> Unit
walkDecls _ [] = ()
walkDecls acc (d::rest) =
  let _ = mapOriginsInDecl (noteHead acc) (noteIfaceOcc acc) d
  walkDecls acc rest

noteHead : Ref (List (String, TyConOrigin)) -> Ty -> (Ty, Bool)
noteHead acc (t@(TyCon { tyConName = n, tyConOrigin = o })) =
  acc := (n, o) :: !acc
  (t, False)
noteHead _ t = (t, False)

-- `noteIfaceOcc` records and hands the origin straight back, so the tree is
-- returned unchanged and the walk is a pure observation — the exact shape
-- `noteHead` already uses.  The carrier tag is not part of the KEY: a `Speak`
-- written as a `=>` and a `Speak` written as an `impl` head name the same
-- declaration, so they belong in the same row and must be able to disagree with
-- each other.  The tag is what `carrierCounts` below asserts on.
noteIfaceOcc : Ref (List (String, TyConOrigin)) -> String -> String -> TyConOrigin -> TyConOrigin
noteIfaceOcc acc _ n o =
  acc := (ifaceKey n, o) :: !acc
  o

-- ── the PER-CARRIER anti-vacuity counts (#1110 PR C) ─────────────────────────
-- 🚨 FOUR counts, not one, and each names its carrier field LITERALLY.  Two things
-- rest on that, and neither is cosmetic:
--
--   * A single merged `iface-occs` total stays healthily large while ONE of the
--     four carriers goes dead — `superOrigin` is the smallest population in every
--     corpus here, so it is exactly the one a merged count would hide.  These are
--     the peer of `decl-heads`, one per carrier.
--   * `test/typecheck_compiler_source.sh`'s carrier ratchet verifies GRADED status
--     by requiring each carrier's field NAME to appear, outside a comment, in THIS
--     FILE.  This probe reads the four through resolve's shared `mapOriginsInDecl`
--     traversal — by position, naming none of them — so without these literals the
--     ratchet could not distinguish "graded through a shared walk" from "not graded
--     at all", and the honest way to satisfy it is to assert per carrier rather
--     than to loosen the pin.
--
-- The walk is its own, driven with an INERT `Ty` callback, so it visits exactly the
-- positions the stamper visits and observes only the interface ones.
ifaceCarriers : List String
ifaceCarriers =
  ["constraintOrigin", "superOrigin", "requireOrigin", "implOrigin"]

carrierCounts : List Decl -> List (String, List Decl) -> List (String, Int)
carrierCounts coreD mods =
  map (carrierCountOf (coreD ++ flatMap snd mods)) ifaceCarriers

carrierCountOf : List Decl -> String -> (String, Int)
carrierCountOf decls tag =
  let acc = Ref 0
  let _ = walkCarrier tag acc decls
  (tag, !acc)

walkCarrier : String -> Ref Int -> List Decl -> Unit
walkCarrier _ _ [] = ()
walkCarrier tag acc (d::rest) =
  let _ = mapOriginsInDecl inertTy (bumpCarrier tag acc) d
  walkCarrier tag acc rest

bumpCarrier : String -> Ref Int -> String -> String -> TyConOrigin -> TyConOrigin
bumpCarrier tag acc carrier _ o =
  if carrier == tag then
    acc := !acc + 1
    o
  else o

inertTy : Ty -> (Ty, Bool)
inertTy t = (t, False)

carrierLines : List (String, Int) -> String
carrierLines [] = ""
carrierLines ((tag, n)::rest) = "iface-occs-\{tag} \{n}\n\{carrierLines rest}"

-- ── the DECL-LAYER carrier collector (#1110) ─────────────────────────────────
declHeads : List Decl -> List (String, TyConOrigin)
declHeads decls = flatMap declHeadOf decls

-- 🚨 EVERY ARM NAMES A LABEL NO SIBLING CONSTRUCTOR HAS, and that is a correctness
-- requirement here, not a style note: a record pattern matches by LABEL SET, not by
-- constructor (`frontend/ast.mdk`'s rule, and the run≠build divergence at exit 0
-- that motivates it).  The four `…Origin` fields are prefixed per constructor, so
-- they are unique BY CONSTRUCTION — which is why each arm keys on the origin field
-- it is here to read rather than on the name field it also needs.
--
-- ⚠️ The `DAttrib` arm is load-bearing for the same reason `stampDeclOrigin`'s is:
-- `@deprecated`/`@inline` WRAP the decl they annotate, so a `@must_use data Foo`
-- would otherwise be observed by neither the stamper's grader nor this probe — the
-- one shape where a stamping bug and its gate would go blind together.
--
-- ⚠️ `DImpl` mints NO decl-layer carrier and must not appear here.  An impl is not
-- a type declaration; the identity of the head it is an impl OF is carried by the
-- `Ty`s in `DImpl.tys`, which the OCCURRENCE walk above already covers (that is
-- what beta.mdk's `impl Weighable (Crate a)` fixture exercises).
declHeadOf : Decl -> List (String, TyConOrigin)
declHeadOf (DData { dataName = n, dataOrigin = o }) = [("decl:\{n}", o)]
declHeadOf (DNewtype { newtypeName = n, newtypeOrigin = o }) =
  [("decl:\{n}", o)]
declHeadOf (DTypeAlias { tyAliasName = n, tyAliasOrigin = o }) =
  [("decl:\{n}", o)]
declHeadOf (DInterface { name = n, ifaceOrigin = o }) = [("decl:\{n}", o)]
declHeadOf (DAttrib _ d) = declHeadOf d
declHeadOf _ = []

declHeadsCount : List (String, List Decl) -> Int
declHeadsCount [] = 0
declHeadsCount ((_, decls)::rest) =
  listLen (declHeads decls) + declHeadsCount rest

-- ── what one arm claims about one head ───────────────────────────────────────

data ArmState = ArmAbsent | ArmSilent | ArmOne String | ArmMulti (List String)

-- ⚠️ `ArmMulti` is ONE arm claiming TWO different origins for the same head in the
-- same slot.  It is not a tie to be broken — it is an internal contradiction, so it
-- conflicts with everything, including an identical `ArmMulti`.
armStateOf : String -> List Claim -> ArmState
armStateOf arm rows = armStateFrom (nubStrs (keysOfArm arm rows))

armStateFrom : List String -> ArmState
armStateFrom [] = ArmAbsent
armStateFrom ["-"] = ArmSilent
armStateFrom [k] = ArmOne k
armStateFrom ks = ArmMulti ks

keysOfArm : String -> List Claim -> List String
keysOfArm _ [] = []
keysOfArm arm (c::rest) =
  if claimArm c == arm then
    claimKey c :: keysOfArm arm rest
  else
    keysOfArm arm rest

showArm : ArmState -> String
showArm ArmAbsent = "absent"
showArm ArmSilent = "-"
showArm (ArmOne k) = k
showArm (ArmMulti ks) = "multi:\{joinPipe ks}"

-- The whole verdict vocabulary, in one place:
--   BOTH-ABSENT           neither arm processed this head at all
--   L-ABSENT / R-ABSENT   one arm never saw it
--   NEITHER               both saw it, neither could attribute it (§8 I6.3 residual)
--   L-ONLY / R-ONLY       one arm claims, the other is honestly SILENT — CORRECT
--                         today for every user declaration on the flat path
--   AGREE                 both claim, same module
--   CONFLICT              both claim, DIFFERENT modules — the defect class
verdict : ArmState -> ArmState -> String
verdict ArmAbsent ArmAbsent = "BOTH-ABSENT"
verdict ArmAbsent _ = "L-ABSENT"
verdict _ ArmAbsent = "R-ABSENT"
verdict ArmSilent ArmSilent = "NEITHER"
verdict ArmSilent _ = "R-ONLY"
verdict _ ArmSilent = "L-ONLY"
verdict (ArmOne a) (ArmOne b) = if a == b then "AGREE" else "CONFLICT"
verdict _ _ = "CONFLICT"

-- ── grouping + rendering ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `uniq` is sorted by (slot, name, arm, key), so each head's claims form ONE
-- contiguous run.  Returns (table, conflict detail, row count, conflicting rows).
groupRows : List Claim -> (String, String, Int, Int)
groupRows [] = ("", "", 0, 0)
groupRows (c::rest) =
  let slot = claimSlot c
  let name = claimName c
  let (rows, after) = spanRow slot name (c::rest)
  let (line, conf, nc) = renderRow slot name rows
  let (lines2, confs2, nrows, nconf) = groupRows after
  ("\{line}\{lines2}", "\{conf}\{confs2}", 1 + nrows, nc + nconf)

spanWhile : (Claim -> Bool) -> List Claim -> (List Claim, List Claim)
spanWhile _ [] = ([], [])
spanWhile p (c::rest) =
  if p c then
    consFst c (spanWhile p rest)
  else
    ([], c::rest)

spanRow : String -> String -> List Claim -> (List Claim, List Claim)
spanRow slot name cs = spanWhile (c => sameRow slot name c) cs

sameRow : String -> String -> Claim -> Bool
sameRow slot name c = claimSlot c == slot && claimName c == name

-- ── residual: the RUNTIME DRAIN (#1110 §8 I6.3) ──────────────────────────────
-- Per arm, which heads still carry `OriginUnresolved` AFTER that driver's stamping
-- pass ran.  This is the half #1110 asked for and the half that was missing: the
-- producer ratchet in `test/typecheck_compiler_source.sh` pins WHO MAY MINT the
-- sentinel, which is a fact about the SOURCE, and its own comment says what that
-- cannot reach —
--
--     "This is a PRODUCER ratchet, not the runtime drain #1110 asks for. It cannot
--      see whether a driver forgot to stamp, nor whether one stamped the WRONG id"
--
-- The second of those two is covered by the CONFLICT rows above.  This section is
-- the first.  Pinning the surviving set in the golden means a driver that FORGETS
-- to stamp a head grows a row here, and `stampTyHead`'s immunity rule (fill in only
-- an `OriginUnresolved` head) is what makes that worth catching: the FIRST stamp is
-- the only one, so a head that reaches a consumer unstamped stays unstamped.
--
-- 🚨 A NONZERO RESIDUAL IS NOT A BUG REPORT, AND THIS SECTION MUST NOT BE READ AS
-- ONE.  `OriginUnresolved` means "no identity was available here", and resolve.mdk
-- names two ways to reach it that are both HONEST ABSENCE: a name in no scope at
-- all, and a driver with no module graph.  The second is a whole ARM of this probe.
-- Concretely, the shapes that are EXPECTED here:
--
--   * every `flat` row.  `stampFlatTyOrigins` claims a strict SUBSET of what the
--     graph path claims — builtins and the prelude's own types, and nothing else.
--     It does not stamp the user program's own declarations (no loader id to
--     attribute them to), it does not walk `usePathsOf prog` so it stamps nothing
--     the buffer IMPORTS either.  The decl layer (`stampDeclOrigins`) is called on
--     the flat arm's PRELUDE half only (`checkProgramSeededSplit`, #1227, fixed),
--     so `flat core decl:*` no longer shows up here — it moved to AGREE in the
--     table above.  `decl:` rows are still silent here for the user program's own
--     declarations, and for any flat driver with no separate prelude boundary
--     (`coreProg0 = []`, e.g. `checkProgramSeeded`) — those have no module id to
--     stamp with, same as the occurrence layer.  Under-supplying identity is
--     correctable by a later graph pass; OVER-supplying a wrong one is made
--     PERMANENT by the immunity rule.  That asymmetry is the documented
--     subset-and-agreement safety property, and these rows ARE it.
--
--   * a `graph` row is the one that deserves reading.  The graph path has a real
--     module id for every module and a scope built from builtins + prelude +
--     imports + own declarations, so a head it left unstamped is either genuinely
--     out of scope (already reported as `UnknownType`; errors accumulate, so the
--     tree still gets here) or a driver gap.  Neither is decidable from this
--     output alone — which is why the set is PINNED for review rather than
--     asserted empty.
--
--     ⚠️ The committed `unresolved` fixture makes the OCCURRENCE zero above
--     earned (falsifiable by mutation-of-fixture) — it proves the graph arm's
--     occurrence walk can emit a row at all.  It proves nothing about the
--     `decl:` layer: `fillDeclOrigin` stamps `OriginUnresolved -> OriginModule
--     mid` unconditionally for any non-empty `mid`, and the loader never mints
--     `""`, so no source program can produce a `graph <slot> decl:X` residual
--     row.  That zero is structural — provable only by mutating the compiler
--     (the stamper, or a post-resolve decl re-synthesis — `d*Unresolved`, the
--     hazard the decl-layer ratchet pins) — not earned by any fixture.  Do not
--     read this section as blanket falsifiable across both layers.
--
-- ⚠️ A head can appear here AND carry a real origin in the same arm and slot; that
-- is `ArmMulti`, an arm contradicting itself, and it is strictly worse than a plain
-- residual.  The verdict table is where that reads as CONFLICT — this section only
-- reports that SOME occurrence of the head survived, never that all of them did.
-- ⚠️ The re-SORT is required (`uniq` is ordered slot-first, this section is ordered
-- arm-first).  The `dedupAdj` is NOT, today: `uniq` is already deduped on
-- (slot, name, arm, key) and this filter fixes `key` to `"-"`, so at most one row
-- per (arm, slot, name) can reach here.  It is kept anyway so this section's output
-- does not silently depend on a keying choice made in `cmpClaim` for the TABLE's
-- benefit — a de-dup that is a no-op is cheap; a duplicated residual row that
-- nobody predicted is a golden diff nobody can explain.
residualSection : List Claim -> (String, Int)
residualSection uniq =
  residualGo (dedupAdj
    sameArmSlotName
    (sortByCmp cmpArmSlotName (silentClaims uniq)))

silentClaims : List Claim -> List Claim
silentClaims [] = []
silentClaims (c::rest) =
  if claimKey c == "-" then
    c :: silentClaims rest
  else
    silentClaims rest

residualGo : List Claim -> (String, Int)
residualGo [] = ("", 0)
residualGo (c::rest) =
  let (s, n) = residualGo rest
  ("\{claimArm c} \{claimSlot c} \{claimName c}\n\{s}", 1 + n)

-- ARM first: the section's whole point is that a `flat` row and a `graph` row mean
-- different things, so they must not interleave.
cmpArmSlotName : Claim -> Claim -> Ordering
cmpArmSlotName a b =
  thenCmp
    (compare (claimArm a) (claimArm b))
    (thenCmp
      (compare (claimSlot a) (claimSlot b))
      (compare (claimName a) (claimName b)))

sameArmSlotName : Claim -> Claim -> Bool
sameArmSlotName a b = claimArm a == claimArm b
  && claimSlot a == claimSlot b
  && claimName a == claimName b

-- ── head spread: the DEFINER-CHASE check ─────────────────────────────────────
-- The agreement table above compares arms WITHIN one slot, so it is structurally
-- blind to one thing the corpus deliberately contains: a type reached ONLY through
-- a RE-EXPORT — one the re-exporting module does not itself declare — must be
-- attributed to its DEFINER, never to the re-exporter
-- (resolve.mdk's `typeOriginExports` exists for exactly this, and it is the half
-- `ModuleExports.expTypes` cannot answer).
--
-- ⚠️ THAT "ONLY" IS LOAD-BEARING, and this comment lacked it until #1245.  When
-- the re-exporting module ALSO DECLARES the name, the definer IS the re-exporter —
-- its own declaration is what a consumer's `import m.{Foo}` binds — and this very
-- section prints that: `Baton 1 mod:mid` and `iface:Relay 1 mod:mid` in
-- `test/origin_fixtures/ifaces/agreement.golden`, where `mid` both re-exports
-- `deep`'s and declares its own.  Unscoped, this header read as a claim its own
-- output contradicts.  If the graph arm attributed `Crate` to
-- `beta` in beta's slot and to `alpha` in alpha's, every row would still read
-- AGREE/R-ONLY and the gate would say nothing.
--
-- So: per head NAME, how many DISTINCT origins does the graph arm claim across all
-- the slots that spell it?  This encodes no answer — it does not say which module
-- is right, only whether the compiler told one story or several.
--
-- ⚠️ PRINT THE KEYS, DO NOT MERELY COUNT THEM.  The count alone is blind to a
-- UNIFORM mis-attribution (see the bound at the top of this file): one wrong story
-- is still one story.  With the keys on the page, relabelling the prelude turns
-- `Option 1 mod:core` into `Option 1 mod:__user__` — F2 on the screen.  Row
-- cardinality is unchanged either way (`rows` is already deduped by (name, key)),
-- so this adds no new golden rot over the counting form.
--
-- ⚠️ `> 1` IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY A DEFECT.  Two unrelated modules declaring
-- same-named types is exactly the case module-qualified identity exists to SUPPORT
-- (#1047), and such a corpus would legitimately read 2.  It is pinned in the golden
-- rather than asserted, so a change here has to be looked at and blessed, which is
-- the honest treatment for a number whose correct value depends on the corpus.
spreadSection : List Claim -> String
spreadSection uniq =
  spreadGo (dedupAdj sameNameKey (sortByCmp cmpNameKey (graphKeyed uniq)))

graphKeyed : List Claim -> List Claim
graphKeyed [] = []
graphKeyed (c::rest) =
  if isGraphClaim c then
    c :: graphKeyed rest
  else
    graphKeyed rest

isGraphClaim : Claim -> Bool
isGraphClaim c = claimArm c == "graph" && claimKey c /= "-"

cmpNameKey : Claim -> Claim -> Ordering
cmpNameKey a b =
  thenCmp
    (compare (claimName a) (claimName b))
    (compare (claimKey a) (claimKey b))

sameNameKey : Claim -> Claim -> Bool
sameNameKey a b = claimName a == claimName b && claimKey a == claimKey b

spreadGo : List Claim -> String
spreadGo [] = ""
spreadGo (c::rest) =
  let name = claimName c
  let (rows, after) = spanWhile (x => claimName x == name) (c::rest)
  "\{name} \{listLen rows} \{joinPipe (map claimKey rows)}\n\{spreadGo after}"

consFst : Claim -> (List Claim, List Claim) -> (List Claim, List Claim)
consFst c (rows, after) = (c::rows, after)

renderRow : String -> String -> List Claim -> (String, String, Int)
renderRow slot name rows =
  let f = armStateOf "flat" rows
  let s = armStateOf "single" rows
  let g = armStateOf "graph" rows
  let vFG = verdict f g
  let vSG = verdict s g
  let vFS = verdict f s
  let bad = vFG == "CONFLICT" || vSG == "CONFLICT" || vFS == "CONFLICT"
  let line = "\{slot} \{name} \{vFG} \{vSG} \{vFS}\n"
  let detail = "\{slot} \{name} flat=\{showArm f} single=\{showArm s} graph=\{showArm g}\n"
  (line, if bad then detail else "", if bad then 1 else 0)

-- ── local list utilities ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Hand-rolled and POLYMORPHIC (one copy, not one per element type): these probe
-- entries are compiled off the compiler root, and the compiler's own mini-stdlib
-- has no sort.  O(n log n) is not optional — the unsorted claim list is one entry
-- per Ty position per arm per module, tens of thousands of rows on a real corpus.

sortByCmp : (a -> a -> Ordering) -> List a -> List a
sortByCmp _ [] = []
sortByCmp _ [x] = [x]
sortByCmp cmp xs =
  let (l, r) = halveL xs
  mergeByCmp cmp (sortByCmp cmp l) (sortByCmp cmp r)

mergeByCmp : (a -> a -> Ordering) -> List a -> List a -> List a
mergeByCmp _ [] ys = ys
mergeByCmp _ xs [] = xs
mergeByCmp cmp (x::xs) (y::ys) = match cmp x y
  Gt => y :: mergeByCmp cmp (x::xs) ys
  _ => x :: mergeByCmp cmp xs (y::ys)

halveL : List a -> (List a, List a)
halveL xs =
  let n = listLen xs / 2
  (takeN n xs, dropN n xs)

takeN : Int -> List a -> List a
takeN _ [] = []
takeN n (x::rest) = if n <= 0 then [] else x :: takeN (n - 1) rest

dropN : Int -> List a -> List a
dropN _ [] = []
dropN n (x::rest) = if n <= 0 then x::rest else dropN (n - 1) rest

dedupAdj : (a -> a -> Bool) -> List a -> List a
dedupAdj _ [] = []
dedupAdj _ [x] = [x]
dedupAdj eq (x::y::rest) =
  if eq x y then
    dedupAdj eq (y::rest)
  else
    x :: dedupAdj eq (y::rest)

-- (slot, name) FIRST so each head's claims form one contiguous run for `groupRows`.
cmpClaim : Claim -> Claim -> Ordering
cmpClaim (Claim a1 s1 n1 k1) (Claim a2 s2 n2 k2) =
  thenCmp
    (compare s1 s2)
    (thenCmp (compare n1 n2) (thenCmp (compare a1 a2) (compare k1 k2)))

thenCmp : Ordering -> Ordering -> Ordering
thenCmp Eq b = b
thenCmp a _ = a

sameClaim : Claim -> Claim -> Bool
sameClaim a b = match cmpClaim a b
  Eq => True
  _ => False

-- The per-head runs are tiny (at most one claim per arm per Ty position), so a
-- first-occurrence nub is cheaper than another sort — and it preserves the sorted
-- key order `cmpClaim` already established, which keeps `ArmMulti` deterministic.
nubStrs : List String -> List String
nubStrs [] = []
nubStrs (x::rest) = x :: nubStrs (dropStr x rest)

dropStr : String -> List String -> List String
dropStr _ [] = []
dropStr x (y::rest) = if x == y then dropStr x rest else y :: dropStr x rest

joinPipe : List String -> String
joinPipe [] = ""
joinPipe [x] = x
joinPipe (x::rest) = "\{x}|\{joinPipe rest}"
